.TH S3AR 1 "August 2026" "S3 Archiver" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
s3ar \- create and extract POSIX PAX archives of S3 objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-c | \-\-create )
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-zstd ]
.RB [ \-f " TARFILE" ]
.I S3...
.br
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-x | \-\-extract | \-\-get )
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-zstd ]
.RB [ \-f " TARFILE" ]
.RI [ S3... ]
.br
.B s3ar \-\-list-objects
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.I S3...
.br
.B s3ar \-\-list-buckets
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RI [ S3... ]
.br
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-h | \-\-help )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B s3ar
is S3 Archiver, a command-line utility for creating POSIX PAX archives from
an S3-compatible object store, extracting those archives back to S3, and
listing live buckets or objects.
.PP
The interface is intentionally tar-like. Archive operations use familiar
.BR tar (1)
options such as
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.BR \-v ,
and selection operands follow tar member-selection semantics. The utility is
not a complete replacement for tar and implements only the options documented
here.
.PP
Archive members use the form
.BR BUCKET/KEY .
Each selected bucket is represented by one
.B BUCKET/
directory member. Object data is streamed between S3 and libarchive without
staging complete objects on the local filesystem.
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
or with
.BR "\-f \-" ,
create writes the archive to standard output and extract reads it from
standard input. Extract with no S3 operand restores all accepted archive
members.
.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Create an archive from one or more S3 selections. Multiple operands are
processed in command-line order. If overlapping operands select an object
more than once, the object is stored once for each matching operand. Bucket
directory members are stored only once.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract ", " \-\-get
Extract an archive into S3. Operands optionally filter archive members.
Objects overwrite existing objects with the same keys, and missing buckets
are created. Multiple matching filters do not upload an archive member more
than once.
.TP
.B \-\-list-objects
List objects selected by one or more S3 operands. Output contains
.B BUCKET/KEY
without an
.B s3://
prefix, one object per line. Bucket entries are not printed, and an empty
bucket produces no output.
.TP
.B \-\-list-buckets
List all bucket names without their objects, one bucket per line. All
positional arguments are ignored. In particular,
.BR s3://asdasd ,
.BR s3://BUCKET/PREFIX ,
and
.B s3://
do not filter the output and do not cause those resources to be accessed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f " " TARFILE ", " \-\-file= TARFILE
Read or write the named archive. This option is valid only with create or
extract. TARFILE must be a local filesystem path or
.BR \- .
Create truncates an existing local file and writes directly to it. If create
fails after opening the file, a partial archive can remain.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
With create and extract, write each successfully archived or restored member
name as
.B BUCKET/KEY
to standard error. The output never includes an
.B s3://
prefix and always uses standard error, regardless of the archive stream.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-list-objects ,
write tab-separated fields to standard output. Each row contains the object
name, byte size, and comma-separated S3 user metadata sorted by name. A hyphen
denotes an object without user metadata. This mode performs one HEAD request
per object.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-list-buckets ,
append a tab-separated
.IB acl= ACL
summary to every bucket name. An ACL unavailable from the endpoint is shown
as
.BR acl=unavailable .
.TP
.B \-\-zstd
With create, write a zstd-compressed archive. Extract automatically detects
plain and zstd-compressed archives; specifying this option requires zstd
input. The option is not valid with
.B \-\-list-objects
or
.BR \-\-list-buckets .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display command usage and exit successfully.
.SH S3 SELECTION
.TP
.B s3://
Select all buckets and their objects.
.TP
.BR s3://BUCKET " or " s3://BUCKET/
Select the named bucket and all its objects. A trailing slash does not change
the selection.
.TP
.BR s3://BUCKET/NAME " or " s3://BUCKET/NAME/
Select the exact object key
.B NAME
and all objects below
.BR NAME/ .
Matching occurs only at a path boundary. For example,
.B photo
matches
.B photo
and
.BR photo/first.jpg ,
but not
.BR photo1.jpg .
.PP
An object selection that matches neither an exact key nor a descendant is an
error. A bucket selection succeeds for an empty bucket. Input operands must
use the
.B s3://
form even though list and verbose output omit that prefix.
.SH ARCHIVE FORMAT
Archives use the restricted POSIX PAX format. Plain and zstd-compressed
archives are supported. Bucket ACL summaries, object ETags, and S3 user
metadata are stored as SCHILY extended attributes:
.PP
.nf
SCHILY.xattr.s3ar.bucket-acl=public-read,custom
SCHILY.xattr.etag=ETAG
SCHILY.xattr.user.NAME=VALUE
.fi
.PP
User metadata is restored to uploaded objects. Stored ETags and bucket ACL
summaries are informational and are not restored. Unsafe object keys or
archive paths containing empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
components are rejected. Archive links and unsupported member types are also
rejected.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B S3AR_ACCESS_KEY
S3 access key. Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_SECRET_KEY
S3 secret key. Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_ENDPOINT
Required S3 endpoint in the form
.BR [http://|https://]HOST[:PORT] .
When the scheme is omitted, HTTPS is used. A single trailing slash is accepted
and removed; URL paths are not accepted.
.TP
.B S3AR_URI_STYLE
S3 addressing style. Accepted values are
.B path
and
.BR virtual .
The default is
.BR path .
.TP
.B S3AR_REGION
S3 region. When the variable is not set, the default is
.BR us-east-1 .
.PP
The bundled libs3 interface does not support temporary session credentials;
.B S3AR_SESSION_TOKEN
is not supported.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The operation or help output completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage or configuration, allocation failure, S3 failure, archive
failure, or output failure.
.PP
Fatal errors and warnings are written to standard error with an
.B s3ar:
prefix.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1)
